message.content:
The quadratic equation \(x^{2}+2x+3=0\) has no real solutions because its discriminant is negative.

Using the quadratic formula:

\[
x=\frac{-b\pm\sqrt{b^{2}-4ac}}{2a}
\]

with \(a=1,\;b=2,\;c=3\):

\[
\begin{aligned}
\Delta &= b^{2}-4ac = 4-12 = -8,\\[4pt]
x &= \frac{-2\pm\sqrt{-8}}{2}
   = \frac{-2\pm i\,\sqrt{8}}{2}
   = \frac{-2\pm i\,2\sqrt{2}}{2}
   = -1 \pm i\sqrt{2}.
\end{aligned}
\]

So the two complex roots are:

\[
\boxed{x=-1+i\sqrt{2}\quad\text{and}\quad x=-1-i\sqrt{2}}
\]
